course: ukrainian-street
lesson: 2
title: Goodbyes, coming in, yes and no
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Saying goodbye in Ukrainian also has a polite and a casual side: do pobAchennya («until we see each other») is the safe goodbye to any grown-up.
explain: At the school gate you shout do zAvtra (see you tomorrow), and at bedtime on a sleepover everyone says DObroi NOchi (good night) or solOdkykh snIv (sweet dreams).
explain: When Sashko's mum opens the door she says ZAkhody! (come in!) and then rozdyHAisya — take your shoes off, because in most Ukrainian homes outdoor shoes stay by the door.
explain: Yes is tak and no is ni; between kids yes is often a lazy AHA and no a quick nE-a, like «uh-huh» and «nope».
fact: do pobAchennya means goodbye, literally «until we see each other»; it is long but always safe with parents, grandparents, teachers and shopkeepers.
fact: do zAvtra means see you tomorrow; zAvtra is tomorrow, and on a Friday people say do ponedIlka, till Monday.
fact: DObroi NOchi means good night and is said only at bedtime, never as an evening hello.
fact: solOdkykh snIv means sweet dreams; mums say it to kids and friends say it at sleepovers, and the answer is DYAkuyu, i tobI! (thanks, you too).
fact: na vsE DObre means all the best or take care, a nice polite goodbye to adults or for a longer parting.
fact: shcho NOvoho? means what's new?, asked between friends; the answer can be nIchoho (nothing) or a piece of news.
fact: ZAkhody! means come in!, what the friend's mum says at the door.
fact: rozdyHAisya means take your shoes off; walking into a Ukrainian flat in outdoor shoes is a real faux pas, and you may be offered slippers, TAPochky.
fact: tak means yes, to anyone; ne tak means «not like that» or «wrong».
fact: ni means no; it sounds like the English word «knee».
fact: AHA is a casual yeah or uh-huh between friends; to a granny tak is better, because AHA can sound lazy.
fact: nE-a is a casual nope between kids, and to adults it can sound rude.
exercise: What does do pobAchennya mean? | Goodbye, until we see each other | Good night, sleep tight | See you tomorrow at school | Come in and sit down
exercise: Who is do pobAchennya safe to say to? | Any grown-up: parents, teachers, shopkeepers | Only your best friend | Only babies and pets | Only people you will never see again
exercise: What does do zAvtra mean? | See you tomorrow | See you next year | Good morning | Goodbye for ever
exercise: When is DObroi NOchi said? | At bedtime | As a hello in the evening | At breakfast | When you arrive at a party
exercise: What does solOdkykh snIv mean? | Sweet dreams | Sweet sweets | Sleep is boring | Wake up now
exercise: How do you answer solOdkykh snIv? | DYAkuyu, i tobI! | DObroho RANku! | ZAkhody, rozdyHAisya! | nE-a!
exercise: What does na vsE DObre mean? | All the best, take care | Everything is broken | All of you, come in | Good evening to everyone
exercise: What does shcho NOvoho? mean? | What's new? | What's your name? | What's for dinner? | What colour is it?
exercise: Sashko's mum opens the door and says ZAkhody! What does she mean? | Come in! | Go away! | Wait outside! | Close the door!
exercise: What should you do when you hear rozdyHAisya at the door? | Take your shoes off | Take your coat to the kitchen | Wash your hands | Say your full name
exercise: What does tak mean? | Yes | No | Maybe | Thanks
exercise: What does ni mean? | No | Knee | Yes | Now
exercise: What is AHA between friends? | A casual yeah, uh-huh | A sneeze | A polite no for grown-ups | A laugh at a joke
exercise: Why is tak better than AHA with a granny? | AHA can sound lazy to her | AHA means no | AHA is a rude word for old people | Grannies don't know AHA
exercise: What does nE-a mean? | Nope (casual no) | Yes, please, very much | Hello there | Not yet
exercise: Why should you avoid nE-a with adults? | It can sound rude | It means yes to adults | It is a swear word | It is only for girls
test: «ni means knee in ukrainian lol» Which is right? | No, ni means no; it only sounds like «knee» | Yes, ni is the body part, the bend in your leg | Yes, and it also means leg | No, ni means yes
test: It's 8 in the evening and you arrive at Sashko's. You say «DObroi NOchi!». What is wrong? | DObroi NOchi is good night at bedtime, not hello | Nothing, it is the evening hello | It is a morning greeting | It means goodbye for ever
test: You walk into the flat in muddy trainers. What would happen? | It would be a real faux pas; shoes come off at the door | Nothing, shoes always stay on indoors | The family would clap | You would be offered outdoor boots
test: It's Friday at the school gate. Which goodbye fits Monday's meeting? | do ponedIlka | do zAvtra | DObroi NOchi | DObroho RANku
test: «shcho NOvoho?» asks Dmytryk. You have no news. What can you say? | nIchoho | ZAkhody | rozdyHAisya | solOdkykh snIv
test: Leaving the friend's flat, what do you say to his granny? | do pobAchennya or na vsE DObre | nE-a | poKA-poKA, babe, see ya tomorrow | AHA, buvai
test: Sashko's mum offers TAPochky after rozdyHAisya. What is she giving you? | Slippers | Biscuits | A towel | A taxi
test: «ne tak!» shouts your friend as you build the den. What does he mean? | Not like that, that's wrong | Yes, perfect, keep going like that | No thanks | Take your shoes off
test: In a game a cartoon boy says «AHA» to the queen when she asks a question. What would be better? | tak | nE-a | zdorOv | ni-ni
test: «solodkykh sniv is soppy, only babies say it» Which is right? | No, in a family it is just a sweet normal goodnight | Yes, only for babies | Yes, it is an insult | No, it means get up
test: «oi stop saying nE-a to my mum, she hates that» Why does the friend say this? | nE-a is a casual nope that can sound rude to adults | nE-a is a swear word | nE-a means yes to adults | His mum doesn't speak Ukrainian, only English and Polish
exam: Identify the valediction appropriate to be addressed by a child to the grandparents of his companion. | do pobAchennya | nE-a | AHA | zdorOv
exam: State the conduct expected of a visitor upon hearing the injunction rozdyHAisya. | The removal of outdoor footwear at the threshold | The removal of the coat and its deposit in the kitchen | The washing of the hands before entry | The announcement of one's full name
exam: Select the response consistent with the established usage of DObroi NOchi. | It is employed solely at the hour of retiring, and never as a salutation upon arrival | It is the customary salutation upon arrival in the evening | It is a greeting reserved for the morning | It is an expression of gratitude for food
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the affirmatives tak and AHA? | Both signify yes, whereas AHA is casual and may appear lazy when addressed to an elder | Both signify no, whereas tak is impolite | tak signifies no and AHA signifies yes | AHA is the more formal of the two
